course: geography
lesson: 40
title: The game map and the real map: what EU5 simplifies
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Europa Universalis 5 is a brilliant map of the years 1337 to 1836, but every game must simplify, the way a football video game does not show the players getting tired, bored or cold.
explain: In the game each location has one owner, one sharp border, one population number and one trade good; in real life a village could owe rent to a lord, tithes to a church and taxes to a king all at once, and its border with the next land might be a vague stretch of forest.
explain: The game's "empty" lands were full of people, its population numbers are historians' best guesses, and its flat map stretches the far north, so Scandinavia, Siberia and Greenland look bigger than they are.
explain: Real armies and letters crawled at walking or riding pace, winter could stop a campaign dead, rivers silted up, the sea took and gave back land, and a storm could wreck a fleet, as it helped wreck the Spanish Armada in 1588.
explain: So play the game and enjoy it, but when it says "one click", remember the real world said "many years, many people, and a lot of luck".
fact: EU5 divides the world into thousands of small locations, but real medieval lands had no neat equal units and their borders were often unclear.
fact: A real medieval village might owe rent to one lord, tithes (a tenth of its harvest) to a church and taxes to a king at the same time.
fact: Real borders in 1337 were often fuzzy zones, and it was only after the 1600s and 1700s that states drew exact border lines.
fact: EU5 shows many lands as empty and waiting to be colonised, but nearly all of them were home to people with their own nations and laws.
fact: A flat map of the round Earth always stretches some places, and the common Mercator map makes Greenland look as big as Africa, though Africa is about 14 times bigger.
fact: In the 1300s news and armies moved at walking or riding pace, and a letter from London to Rome could take a month or more.
fact: Supply, weather and winter often stopped real campaigns altogether.
fact: Population numbers in EU5 are estimates, because there were few real censuses, official counts of the people, in 1337 and historians argue about the figures; England's Domesday Book of 1086, a count of land and households made for taxes, was an unusual record for its time.
fact: EU5 gives each location one main trade good, but real places produced many things at once, from grain and wool to cloth and fish.
fact: Place names in games are often modern or English versions, while people at the time used other names, such as Constantinople for Istanbul or Tenochtitlan for the site of Mexico City.
fact: Beijing was called Khanbaliq or Dadu under the Yuan in 1337.
fact: Coastlines and rivers have changed since 1337: the Dutch have reclaimed large areas from the sea, and some medieval ports have silted up, as Bruges did when trade moved to Antwerp.
fact: In EU5 rulers make choices with clear results, but real history was shaped also by weather, disease, harvests and chance.
exercise: Did medieval countries have neat equal provinces like a game? | No, their lands were uneven and borders often unclear | Yes, every land was cut into equal squares by the king's surveyors | Yes, kings measured them with satellites | No, there were no lands at all
exercise: Why were medieval borders often fuzzy? | Lands faded into one another and few exact lines were drawn | Everyone had agreed on exact lines at a great meeting of kings in 1337 | Borders were painted on the ground | Borders were set by the UN
exercise: When did states start drawing exact border lines? | Mostly after the 1600s and 1700s | Long before the Romans, in the Stone Age | In 1337 exactly, all at the same time | Only after the year 2000, with satellites
exercise: To whom might a medieval village pay at the same time? | A lord, a church and a king | Only the Pope in Rome and his bishops | Only the nearest town council | Nobody at all, ever, in any year
exercise: Why is "colonising an empty province" not what really happened? | Nearly all such lands already had people with their own nations | The provinces really were empty, just as the game shows | Colonists always asked politely and paid a fair price for every field | Colonising meant planting trees
exercise: Is Greenland really as big as Africa? | No, Africa is about 14 times bigger | Yes, they are almost exactly the same size | No, Greenland is actually a little bigger | No, Africa is only about twice as big
exercise: Why does the Mercator map make Greenland look huge? | A flat map stretches places far from the Equator | Greenland's ice sheet is so thick that it spreads out over the sea | Mapmakers liked Greenland | Greenland is closer to the camera
exercise: How long might a letter take from London to Rome in the 1300s? | A month or more | About an hour by horse | About a day or two | About ten years or so
exercise: What often stopped real campaigns altogether? | Supply, weather and winter | Lack of video screens | The Security Council | Speed cameras
exercise: Why do historians disagree about how many people lived in 1337? | There were few real censuses, so numbers are estimates | Everybody was counted twice, once by the lord and once by the church | People refused to be born | All the records are in EU5
exercise: What was the Domesday Book of 1086? | A count of English households for taxes, unusual for its time | A storybook about the end of the world, read aloud in churches on feast days | A list of Venetian ships | A map of America
exercise: Did a real province produce just one thing? | No, it made many things, such as grain, wool, cloth and fish | Yes, only one good each, chosen by the king and never changed | Yes, only gold | No, it made nothing
exercise: What was Istanbul called in 1337? | Constantinople | Tenochtitlan | Khanbaliq | Alexandria on the Bosporus
exercise: What was Beijing called under the Yuan? | Khanbaliq or Dadu | Edo or Tokyo | Tabriz | Agra or Delhi
exercise: Why did Bruges stop being a great port? | Its channel to the sea silted up | The sea rose and drowned it | Napoleon banned all ships from sailing there | It moved to Spain
exercise: How have the Dutch changed their coastline since 1337? | They have reclaimed large areas from the sea | They have let the sea take half the country | They have moved the country inland | They have not changed it at all
exercise: Besides rulers' choices, what else shaped real history? | Weather, disease, harvests and chance | Only the rulers' choices | Only the borders drawn on the maps | Only the game's own rules
test: My mate says "in EU5 I took this province in 3 months, so armies were fast back then." What is wrong? | Real armies walked, needed supplies and were often stopped by winter | Nothing, real armies were faster than cars and could cross Europe in a day | Armies took buses in 1337 | Real wars always lasted one day
test: The storm of 1588 that wrecked parts of the Spanish Armada shows what? | Luck and weather shaped real events | Rulers controlled everything, even the weather | Storms never mattered | The Armada was only a video game
test: On a Mercator map Greenland looks as big as Africa. What would you see on a globe? | Africa is about 14 times bigger | Greenland is a little bit bigger | They are exactly the same size on a globe | Africa is only about twice as big
test: oi the game says this village makes "wool" only. that legit? | No, real places made many things at once | Yes, each village made one thing only and bought the rest | Yes, wool was the only good in 1337 | No, villages made nothing
test: A history book says "In 1337, 4,213,560 people lived in England." What should you think? | It is a guess dressed as an exact number, since there was no census | It is exact, because the king's clerks counted every single person by name | It is far too big, England was empty | It must come from the Domesday Book
test: A medieval merchant from Bruges in 1337 time-travels to today. Why can't he sail home to his port? | Its channel silted up and the sea is far away now | Bruges was moved to France, stone by stone, after a war | Ships are banned in Belgium | Bruges is under the sea now
test: In EU5 you see a sharp black line between two kingdoms in 1337. A real traveller crosses there. What is he most likely to find? | Forest and villages with no clear line at all | A tall fence with guards checking everyone's passports | A painted line on the road | A Schengen sign
test: Why might a village in 1337 be shown under three different rulers in three different books? | It owed rent, tithes and taxes to a lord, a church and a king | Books always lie | Villages moved from one kingdom to another every year with their animals | Only one ruler existed then
test: you're playing as the Yuan in 1337, where's your capital and what's it called then | Beijing, then called Khanbaliq or Dadu | Istanbul, then called Constantinople | Mexico City, then called Tenochtitlan | Agra, then called Hampi
test: A friend says a game map is exactly the real world because experts made it. What is the best answer? | Every map simplifies, and sharp borders and empty lands were not real | Yes, the game is a perfect copy of the real world in every single detail | No, the game invents every single place and every name it shows | Yes, the only mistake is that the sea is the wrong colour
test: If EU5 added winter stopping campaigns, slow letters and uneven harvests, how would the game feel? | Slower and more unpredictable, more like real history | Exactly the same as now | Faster and easier than before, with shorter wars and more battles | It would have no wars at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the boundaries of states in the year 1337? | They frequently constituted indeterminate zones rather than precise lines | They were delineated with exactitude in every instance | They were fixed by international treaty in the twentieth century by the League of Nations | They did not exist in any form
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the Mercator projection represents Greenland as comparable in extent to Africa. | The projection distends regions remote from the Equator | Greenland is in fact of equal area to Africa | The Greenland ice sheet increases its apparent area | Africa is depicted at a reduced scale by convention
exam: Identify the principal reason for scholarly disagreement concerning the population of Europe in 1337. | Comprehensive censuses were seldom conducted, so figures are estimates | Every inhabitant was recorded twice, by the lord and by the parish | Contemporary records are complete but secret | Population was not subject to change in that period, and was never counted
exam: State the cause whereby the port of Bruges ceased to be a principal centre of maritime trade. | The silting of its channel to the sea | The submersion of the city beneath the sea | A prohibition on shipping issued by Napoleon | The relocation of the city to the Kingdom of Spain
